  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does an air conditioner make cold air?",
      "a": "It blows room air over a cold coil where refrigerant is boiling. Boiling takes in heat, so the coil pulls heat out of the air, and cooler air comes out.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the cold come from in an air conditioner?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the compressor do?",
      "a": "The compressor squeezes the refrigerant gas, which makes it much hotter. Being hotter than outside air lets it dump the heat outdoors.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does an air conditioner need a compressor?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an air conditioner blow hot air outside?",
      "a": "That hot air is the heat taken from your room plus the energy the compressor used. The outdoor unit has to get rid of it.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the outdoor unit hot?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an air conditioner drip water?",
      "a": "When warm, humid room air touches the cold coil, its water vapour condenses into droplets, just like on a cold drink. A pipe drains that water away.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the water from an air conditioner come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is an air conditioner like a fridge?",
      "a": "Yes. Both use the same refrigeration cycle to pump heat from a cold place to a warmer one. A fridge cools a small box; an air conditioner cools a room.",
      "asks": [
        "How is an air conditioner similar to a fridge?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Willis Carrier designed one of the first modern air-conditioning systems in 1902, to control humidity in a printing plant.",
    "Air conditioners also dry the air, because water vapour condenses on the cold indoor coil."
  ],
